import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"mlopt on the fragment tower — domain vocabularies as Language fragments","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"mlopt on the fragment tower — domain vocabularies as `Language` fragments","type":"adr","date":"2026-10-24T00:00:00.000Z","status":"proposed","applies_to":"`graphopt`, `kernelopt`, `eqsat`, `gamekit`, `kernel`"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0157-mlopt-on-fragment-tower.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0157-mlopt-on-fragment-tower.md"}'),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0157-mlopt-on-fragment-tower.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0157-mlopt-on-the-fragment-tower-—-domain-vocabularies-as-language-fragments" tabindex="-1">ADR-0157: mlopt on the fragment tower — domain vocabularies as <code>Language</code> fragments <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0157-mlopt-on-the-fragment-tower-—-domain-vocabularies-as-language-fragments" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0157: mlopt on the fragment tower — domain vocabularies as Language fragments”">​</a></h1><p>(<code>handl-language</code>, <code>hnl-engine</code>), <code>egraph</code>, <code>host</code> (<code>host-tensor</code>), <code>engine-transform-rewrite</code>, <code>engine-typecheck-nbe</code></p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>The mlopt discovery stack (eqsat substrate + graphopt tensor domain + kernelopt codegen domain + gamekit play layer) measured its own boundary this week and named it honestly: <strong>the machinery is domain-agnostic, but the generator space is hand-drawn.</strong> Every producer the machine searches is a designed template:</p><ul><li>the op vocabulary is a fixed Python registry (<code>op_def</code> in <code>mlopt_core</code> / sink <code>_IR_TO_TORCH</code> tables);</li><li>law schema classes (<code>fold:*</code>, <code>mixed:*</code>, census mints) are hand-declared Python families (<code>mlopt_discovery.pipeline</code>, <code>handler_synth._FAMILIES</code>);</li><li>handler grammars are fixed body templates (<code>ew</code>/<code>norm</code>/<code>contract</code>/ <code>scan</code>);</li><li>minted <code>synop_*</code> primitives register — but only into a Python-side map, and nothing downstream composes over them as mintable atoms.</li></ul><p>Measured consequence (<code>docs/reports/primitives_finds.md</code>, <code>prim_handlers.md</code> in graphopt): machine-originals appeared <em>only</em> when the vocabulary grew — 21/24 <code>synop_*</code> folds classify machine-original — and the residual frontier is exactly the shapes the hand-drawn grammar cannot express (<code>select</code>/<code>linear</code>-bearing bodies refuse at the handler gate). Composition inside a fixed vocabulary is derivable-by-construction and provably cannot yield originality.</p><p>The principled fix is the platform&#39;s own thesis: <strong>a vocabulary is a <code>Language</code> fragment set; engines dispatch on data; a foreign producer emits fragments the engines execute unchanged</strong> (<code>docs/site/explanation/language-as-data.md</code>). If the discovery stack sits on that tower, minting <em>is</em> fragment emission and vocabulary growth <em>is</em> the ordinary fragment merge — one mechanism at every morphism level instead of per-level Python redesign. Conversely: if the flagship research stack cannot live on the fragment tower, the language-as-data claim is unproven on its hardest corpus.</p><h3 id="verified-current-state" tabindex="-1">Verified current state <a class="header-anchor" href="#verified-current-state" aria-label="Permalink to “Verified current state”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>handl-egraph</code> (<code>egraph/</code> repo) is the ported <code>catopt_core.egraph</code> spec — union-find + congruence closure + compiled matcher + deterministic extraction + provenance, over the kernel&#39;s <code>Term</code>/<code>Value</code>.</li><li><code>mlopt-handl-peer</code> (graphopt <code>packages/mlopt-handl-peer</code>) is a working PyO3 seam: a mlopt fragment directory loads as a handl <code>Language</code>, all shipped laws load, <code>RewriteEngine</code> fires a rule at a named node, and the result agrees with <code>EGraph.apply_rule</code> (<code>tests/test_handl_peer.py</code>).</li><li><code>host-tensor</code> exists as a handl package (<code>modules: [&quot;tensor&quot;]</code>); the numeric contract it must satisfy is mlopt&#39;s fp64 verify leg.</li><li>mlopt-side serializations already exist: <code>law_to_data</code>/<code>law_from_data</code>, handler mint records, <code>_FUSION_SCHEMAS</code> tables, <code>shipped_primitives</code><code>_RECORDS</code> — the vocabulary is <em>already</em> data; it just lives in Python-side registries instead of a <code>Language</code>.</li><li>The discovery loop (<code>bench/drivers/overnight.py</code> + kernelopt&#39;s port) is engine-parameterized: corpora <code>zoo-real</code>/<code>zoo-xmodule</code>/<code>zoo-prim</code> are already pluggable pools; <code>engine=</code> already selects Python vs optional Rust (<code>mlopt-native</code>) backends.</li></ul><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p>Migrate the mlopt stack onto the fragment tower in five phases, in dependency order — fragments first (data layer), bridges second, semantics last. Each phase ships standalone value; none gates the discovery loop on the next.</p><h3 id="phase-0-—-canonical-fragment-vocabulary" tabindex="-1">Phase 0 — canonical fragment vocabulary <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-0-—-canonical-fragment-vocabulary" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 0 — canonical fragment vocabulary”">​</a></h3><p>Emit the discovery vocabulary as a fragment directory — one <code>Language</code>-shaped fragment set covering: op declarations (<code>op_def</code>-equivalent rows), the law bank (<code>law_to_data</code> rows), handler specs, <code>_FUSION_SCHEMAS</code>, <code>shipped_primitives._RECORDS</code>, and the handler-grammar family tables. The fragment dir becomes the single source of truth; the Python registries are <em>generated from</em> it (or read it at import). Exit: <code>mlopt-handl-peer</code> loads the same dir both engines use; a fragment added by hand or by machine is visible to both.</p><h3 id="phase-1-—-handl-as-a-first-class-engine" tabindex="-1">Phase 1 — handl as a first-class engine <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-1-—-handl-as-a-first-class-engine" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 1 — handl as a first-class engine”">​</a></h3><p><code>engine=&quot;handl&quot;</code> seat beside the Python e-graph and <code>mlopt-native</code>: laws execute through <code>handl-egraph</code>/<code>RewriteEngine</code>; the Python engine remains the differential oracle (<code>handl_differential.py</code> pattern — two engines, one must agree or the run refuses). Exit: an overnight arm runs <code>--engine handl</code> with parity stats; divergence is a loud refusal, never a silent difference.</p><h3 id="phase-2-—-denotations-as-models" tabindex="-1">Phase 2 — denotations as Models <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-2-—-denotations-as-models" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 2 — denotations as Models”">​</a></h3><p><code>synop_*</code> bodies and handler mints become handl handler records over <code>host-tensor</code>. <code>TorchSink</code> remains the delivery path and the fp64 oracle until parity is measured; a minted op whose Model verifies is &quot;handl-shipped&quot;, otherwise Python-shipped — provenance records which.</p><h3 id="phase-3-—-mint-emits-fragments-the-fix" tabindex="-1">Phase 3 — mint emits fragments (the fix) <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-3-—-mint-emits-fragments-the-fix" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 3 — mint emits fragments (the fix)”">​</a></h3><p>The mint&#39;s outputs become fragment emissions merged into the ambient <code>Language</code>: a mined recurring shape emits an op row + denotation fragment; a refused-spec analysis emits a handler-family fragment; antiunify over failed mints emits schema fragments. The generator space is then corpus-derived by construction — <strong>the bank grows under play at every morphism level</strong>, which is the stated research claim (&quot;a bank that grows under play&quot;) finally holding for generators, not only rules.</p><h3 id="phase-4-—-play-and-saturation-over-the-tower" tabindex="-1">Phase 4 — play and saturation over the tower <a class="header-anchor" href="#phase-4-—-play-and-saturation-over-the-tower" aria-label="Permalink to “Phase 4 — play and saturation over the tower”">​</a></h3><p><code>Board</code> seats whose move space is fragment application; <code>handl-egraph</code> becomes the saturation engine pending the perf-parity gate; law guards (<code>check</code>/<code>derive</code>/<code>cond</code>) migrate toward type-level predicates as <code>engine-typecheck-nbe</code> matures (ADR-0152/0153/0154 lineage).</p><h2 id="alternatives-considered" tabindex="-1">Alternatives considered <a class="header-anchor" href="#alternatives-considered" aria-label="Permalink to “Alternatives considered”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Tower discipline in Python only</strong> — give each generator a <code>{schema, merge, dispatch-on-data}</code> triple without migrating. Cheaper, fixes the search problem, but leaves the language-as-data thesis unproven on the flagship corpus and forks the vocabulary schema from the platform&#39;s.</li><li><strong>Big-bang rewrite in handl</strong> — rewrite the loop in <code>.hnl</code>. Rejected: no measured benefit over fragments-first, and it discards the working Python oracle that the differential checker depends on.</li><li><strong>Status quo</strong> — per-generator Python patches. Rejected by the measured evidence: each hand-drawn level (families, schemas) is precisely where the machine stalls.</li></ul><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li>The fragment dir is the contract: schema evolution there is a breaking change for both engines — version it like <code>law_to_data</code>.</li><li><code>host-tensor</code> fp64 parity and <code>handl-egraph</code> saturation perf are the two hard gates; both are measurable before any semantic migration.</li><li>The discovery loop gains a new honesty class: a mint that emits a malformed fragment is refused at the <code>Language</code> merge, not at a Python try/except — schema-validity is checked once, structurally.</li><li>kernelopt (private) follows the same phases; its kernel-vocabulary fragments stay repo-private per the visibility closure (ADR-0150).</li></ul><h2 id="open-questions" tabindex="-1">Open questions <a class="header-anchor" href="#open-questions" aria-label="Permalink to “Open questions”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Whether law guards should become type predicates (Phase 4) or stay a separate <code>cond</code> fragment class — depends on the typecheck-nbe roadmap&#39;s dependent-eliminator surface.</li><li>Whether <code>shipped_primitives</code>/<code>MACHINE_SHIPPED</code> promotion stays a Python-side registry entry or becomes a fragment tag — decide at Phase 0 with the provenance owner.</li><li>Cross-repo versioning of the fragment schema while <code>eqsat</code>/ <code>graphopt</code>/<code>kernelopt</code> pin independently — the <code>uv.lock</code> bump pattern already exists; a fragment schema bump must ride it loudly.</li></ul>`,27)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};